  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Open Societies Movement to Reclaim Democracy, Human Rights, and Rule of Law&#x201d; Programme in Indonesia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme is a strategic initiative designed to reinforce civil society actors (CSAs) in their efforts to safeguard democratic governance, human rights, and the rule of law. The programme adopts a rapid, context-driven approach to support coalitions working on election law reform, criminal justice (KUHAP), and inclusive human rights engagement. The programme aims to build a coordinated movement by strengthening existing CSAs and coalitions, rather than creating new entities. It engages national and subnational partners to influence legislation, protect civic space, and promote inclusive participation. Activities include context assessments using political economy analysis (PEA). These assessments inform strategic planning and advocacy efforts. Key components include coalition funding for advocacy and partnerships with local organisations including research and legal aid institutions as well as grassroot media. The programme supports campaigns, public discussions, and participatory monitoring, aiming to transform technical legal discourse into accessible public narratives and policy recommendations. A strategy and learning hub (movement lab) will facilitate adaptive management, enabling CSAs to test and refine approaches in response to political developments. The programme also applies an Issue-Based Programming (IBP) approach to align with reform opportunities, ensuring inclusive engagement across gender, age, and identity groups. Through coordinated action, evidence-based advocacy, and inclusive dialogue, the programme aspires to contribute to a more resilient civil society and a strengthened democratic framework in Indonesia.</narrative>
  </description>
